-- title:
--   Lemma 3 — $I(\omega=0\mid\zeta_1(\alpha))\le n\alpha$
-- statement:
--   Let $(X,\nu)$ be a probability space carrying a state $\omega\in\{0,1\}$ and, for each expert $i\in[n]$, an information $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal G_i$ and a forecast $F_i$ that is $\mathcal G_i$-measurable and a version of $\mathbb P(\omega=1\mid\mathcal G_i)$, i.e. $\nu(\{\omega=1\}\cap E)=\int_E F_i\,d\nu$ for every $E\in\mathcal G_i$. This is an arbitrary information structure $I$ (not necessarily a partial evidence one): in the paper $X=\Omega\times S_1\times\dots\times S_n$, $\mathcal G_i$ is generated by $s_i$ and $F_i=I(\omega=1,s_i)/(I(\omega=1,s_i)+I(\omega=0,s_i))$. For $\alpha>0$ let
--
--   $$
--   \zeta_0(\alpha)=\{\exists i:\ F_i\le\alpha\},\qquad \zeta_1(\alpha)=\{\exists i:\ F_i\ge1-\alpha\}
--   $$
--
--   be the events that some expert's forecast is extreme low, resp. extreme high.
--
--   **Lemma 3.** For every $\alpha>0$,
--
--   $$
--   \nu\bigl(\{\omega=0\}\cap\zeta_1(\alpha)\bigr)\le n\alpha\,\nu\bigl(\zeta_1(\alpha)\bigr),
--   $$
--
--   i.e. $I(\omega=0\mid\zeta_1(\alpha))\le n\alpha$. It is the mirror image of Lemma 2 for an extreme high forecast.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: information structure I ∈ Δ(Ω × S_1 × … × S_n) with F_i = I(ω = 1, s_i)/(I(ω = 1, s_i) + I(ω = 0, s_i)) → any probability space with forecasts F_i that are 𝓖_i-measurable versions of P(ω = 1 | 𝓖_i) for sub-σ-algebras 𝓖_i; reason: the paper's structure is the case 𝓖_i = σ(s_i)
--   - reading: ζ_1(α): "at least one expert's forecast is in [1, 1 − α]" → ∃ i, F_i ≥ 1 − α; reason: [1, 1 − α] is empty for α > 0; [1 − α, 1] is the only nonempty reading and the one used on pp.20–21
--   - encoding: I(ω = 0 | ζ_1(α)) ≤ nα, with α > 0 from Lemma 2 → ν({ω = 0} ∩ ζ_1(α)) ≤ nα · ν(ζ_1(α)) for α > 0; reason: product form of the conditional probability; Lemma 3 is stated as the analogue of Lemma 2 and inherits its quantifier
-- source:
--   Babichenko, Garber, Learning of Optimal Forecast Aggregation in Partial Evidence Environments, arXiv:1802.07107v1, Lemma 3, p.20

import Mathlib

namespace ForecastAggLearn

open MeasureTheory

/-- **Lemma 3: an extreme high forecast caps the probability of ω = 0.**
Babichenko, Garber, arXiv:1802.07107v1, Lemma 3, p.20: with `ζ_1(α)` the event "where at least
one expert's forecast is in [1, 1 − α]", "I(ω = 0 | ζ_1(α)) ≤ nα."

**Formalization Note.** "[1, 1 − α]" is read as `[1 − α, 1]`, the only nonempty reading and the
one used on pp.20–21. The setting is that of `lemma_2`: a probability space with the state `ω`
and each forecast `F i` a `𝓖 i`-measurable version of `P(ω = 1 | 𝓖 i)`. The conditional
probability is stated in product form `ν({ω = 0} ∩ ζ_1) ≤ nα · ν(ζ_1)`; the paper's
"for every α > 0" (Lemma 2) is kept. -/
theorem lemma_3 {X : Type*} [mX : MeasurableSpace X] (ν : Measure X) [IsProbabilityMeasure ν]
    (ω : X → Bool) (hω : Measurable ω) {n : ℕ} (𝓖 : Fin n → MeasurableSpace X)
    (h𝓖 : ∀ i, 𝓖 i ≤ mX) (F : Fin n → X → ℝ) (hFm : ∀ i, Measurable[𝓖 i] (F i))
    (hF : ∀ i, ∀ E, MeasurableSet[𝓖 i] E →
      ν ({x | ω x = true} ∩ E) = ∫⁻ x in E, ENNReal.ofReal (F i x) ∂ν)
    (α : ℝ) (hα : 0 < α) :
    ν ({x | ω x = false} ∩ {x | ∃ i, 1 - α ≤ F i x}) ≤
      ENNReal.ofReal (n * α) * ν {x | ∃ i, 1 - α ≤ F i x} := by sorry

end ForecastAggLearn
